Leipzig a city on the move and busier  than ever since reunification the  historical city centre has been restored  the world-famous given to us the trade  fair complex there are plenty of reasons  to visit Leipzig and lots of history the  city held its breath in the autumn of  1989 the nikolaikirche was the beating  heart of the Monday marches that  ultimately culminated in the end of the  communist regime and the reunification  of the country the pivotal role played  by the church is recounted in Carvin  travelers guided tour taking in key  local sights of the peaceful revolution  this church didn't become famous because  of its architecture in 1982 it began  holding special Monday prayers over the  next few years the prayers attracted a  growing number of people who were  opposed to how society was developing in  what was then East Germany the result  was the Monday demonstrations Calvin  trebla was a witness to history she took  part in the protests the forum of  contemporary history gives visitors a  comprehensive picture of Germany during  the Cold War it shows how the one-party  communist government and the East came  into being a museum illustrates what  life was like for East Germans from  communist style competition and Industry  to a typical families living room  1989 marked the beginning at the end as  the party top brass celebrated the  state's 40th birthday the system was  already crumbling beneath them the  Monday demonstrations began in September  by October the 9th the number of  protesters was overwhelming the  authorities considered using physical  force there were seven or eight thousand  armed men in and around the center of  live see between seventy and a hundred  thousand protesters were expected the  regular police and Stasi forces would  have been able to tackle thirty to forty  thousand but not seventy or eighty  thousand the demonstrators chanted no  violence it was dangerous to film the  activities troyler takes her tour to the  place where two activists shot illegal  footage he's alive for me Atticus this  church is precisely the place where the  first film was shot about Monday  protests the material was then passed on  to a journalist from West Germany this  journalist smuggled this little reel of  film to the West in his underwear in a  very discreet place and he got through  the fear was that the secret police the  Stasi would catch him and keep the  recording LaRhonda iike the round corner  is the former Leipzig headquarters of  the Stasi it was occupied by residents  in December 1989 to prevent the  authorities destroying more  incriminating files by the time they  arrived however many documents were  already pop the building now houses a  museum about the Stasi among the  exhibits an old phone tapping device and  a machine for opening letters the museum  reveals the sinister workings of East  Germany's infamous secret police tobias  holitzer was one of the civilian  activists because I know  in the very first night we found a meter  high pile of sacks filled with mail were  we in a post office or at the Stasi and  that's when I realized just what the  Stasi was and my gut reaction was it  must be definitely dissolved definitely  off reduce the stassi's tasks included  monitoring and intimidating its own  citizens but it did not ultimately use  mass physical force against  demonstrators Augustus Platz was the  epicenter of the protests a peaceful  revolution with not a single life lost  that's something we can be absolutely  happy about and grateful for the  opportunity to experience it how often  do you experience something like that  taking a tour with Clarion Troy blur is  just one of the many options and Leipzig  for rediscovering modern German history
 

